      "description": "There was originally a temple in front of the Xiangtangshan Grottoes. The Changle Temple ruins under the Beixiangtang Grottoes are the largest existing ones. Changle Temple was built in the Northern Qi Dynasty and was originally named Grotto Temple. During the Tiantong period of the Northern Qi Dynasty (565-569), it was renamed Zhizhi Temple. During the Jiayou period of the Northern Song Dynasty (1056-1063), it was renamed Changle Temple. It used to be known as \"the first ancient temple along the river\". Changle Temple is a temple complex with a north-south direction. From south to north, there are the Shanmen, the Tianwang Hall, the Third Buddha Hall, the Main Hall and the Ksitigarbha Hall, forming a central axis. There is a Song Pagoda on the south side, called Pu Tong Pagoda, and there is a Buddha Hall echoing it.\nThe original material is provided by Xiaolu（小绿）, the blogger of Xiaohongshu."
